---
import ContentPage from '../components/ContentPage.astro';
import FaqList from '../components/FaqList.astro';
import RelatedLinks from '../components/RelatedLinks.astro';
import { articleSchemas, type FaqItem } from '../seo';
import { SITE_PAGES } from '../site-content';
import { REPO_URL } from '../consts';

const page = SITE_PAGES.storeCoverage;
const breadcrumbs = [{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: page.label, path: page.path }];
const faqs: FaqItem[] = [
  {
    question: 'Does NorthCinder search every store on the internet?',
    answer: 'No. It searches the adapters and permitted product observations available to the buyer. The response reports searched, blocked, unavailable, and unconfigured stores.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can a browser-observed offer be used for automated checkout?',
    answer: 'Not by observation alone. Browser-sourced offers can be compared, but a native adapter or merchant protocol must confirm the offer before automated checkout or an unattended price watch.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why do some adapters require separate approval?',
    answer: 'Each commerce platform controls its production APIs and eligibility. Source code and fixtures prove the adapter contract, not that the operator has current platform credentials or production access.',
  },
];
const schemas = articleSchemas(page, breadcrumbs, faqs);
---

<ContentPage
  page={page}
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  schemas={schemas}
  intro="NorthCinder searches only the store connections and permitted observations available to the buyer. It does not describe a partial result as a complete market search. Every response includes coverage states such as searched, blocked, unavailable, or not configured. Adapter source and fixtures show how an integration behaves; they do not prove that a platform has granted current production credentials to a particular operator."
>
  <h2>Current adapter matrix</h2>
  <p>
    The table separates code capability from platform-controlled access. Requirements can change, so follow
    the linked platform documentation before relying on an integration in production.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll coverage-table-wrap">
    <table class="data-table coverage-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Store</th><th>Current NorthCinder path</th><th>External boundary</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Store">Shopify</td>
          <td data-label="NorthCinder path">Catalog search after additional Shopify setup</td>
          <td data-label="External boundary">Current storefront and platform configuration</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Store">WooCommerce</td>
          <td data-label="NorthCinder path">Public Store API catalog search where a store exposes it</td>
          <td data-label="External boundary">The merchant controls whether that public endpoint exists</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Store">eBay</td>
          <td data-label="NorthCinder path">Native Buy API search</td>
          <td data-label="External boundary">Approved eBay production access is required</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Store">Etsy</td>
          <td data-label="NorthCinder path">Native Etsy API search</td>
          <td data-label="External boundary">An approved Etsy application and API key are required</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Store">Amazon</td>
          <td data-label="NorthCinder path">Read-only comparison through a buyer-controlled browser profile</td>
          <td data-label="External boundary">Stops at challenges; no automated checkout</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Shopify</h2>
  <p>
    NorthCinder's Shopify adapter supports catalog search after the operator completes the current Shopify
    setup. The older assumption that every shop exposes the same per-store connection is no longer used. The
    adapter reports configuration and upstream failures instead of converting them into an empty successful
    search. Review Shopify's current <a href="https://shopify.dev/docs/apps/build/storefront-mcp/servers/storefront">Storefront MCP server documentation</a>
    before configuring a shop.
  </p>

  <h2>WooCommerce</h2>
  <p>
    WooCommerce stores can expose a public Store API intended for customer-facing catalog and cart behavior.
    NorthCinder can search stores where that endpoint is available. The merchant decides whether the endpoint
    exists and what it returns. A successful request to one WooCommerce store says nothing about another
    store. See the <a href="https://developer.woocommerce.com/docs/apis/store-api/">WooCommerce Store API documentation</a>
    for the platform contract.
  </p>

  <h2>eBay</h2>
  <p>
    Native eBay search uses the Buy APIs and requires platform-approved production access. A developer key or
    a fixture alone is not proof of that approval. NorthCinder returns a blocked or unavailable state when the
    configured credentials cannot perform the requested search. eBay documents the current eligibility path
    in its <a href="https://developer.ebay.com/api-docs/buy/buy-requirements.html">Buy API requirements</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Etsy</h2>
  <p>
    Etsy API requests require an approved application and API key. The adapter validates and normalizes the
    product facts NorthCinder needs, but Etsy controls access tiers, reviews, and current terms. Operators
    should check the <a href="https://developers.etsy.com/documentation/">official Etsy developer documentation</a>
    before making a support claim.
  </p>

  <h2>Amazon</h2>
  <p>
    Amazon comparison is deliberately read only. It can use a browser profile already controlled by the
    buyer's agent, observes only the product facts needed for comparison, and stops when it encounters a
    challenge. It does not imitate a human to bypass a challenge and it does not check out. This engineering
    boundary is not a statement that Amazon has granted general authorization for the workflow.
  </p>

  <h2>Browser observations and confirmation</h2>
  <p>
    An AI app may already control a browser and pass permitted product facts to NorthCinder. Those candidates
    are labeled agent-observed. Caller-supplied ranking scores, trust conclusions, sponsorship conclusions,
    cookies, HTML, screenshots, headers, credentials, and page instructions are rejected. The normalized facts
    still pass through schema validation, trust derivation, deterministic ranking, client verification, and
    the local audit path.
  </p>
  <p>
    Observation is sufficient for comparison, not for unattended action. A native adapter or merchant
    protocol must confirm the offer before NorthCinder can authorize automated checkout or attach a background
    price watch. This prevents a transient or manipulated page observation from becoming purchase authority.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      Coverage is a result, not a marketing claim. A useful answer says which stores were searched and which
      were missing so the buyer can decide whether the comparison is broad enough.
    </p>
  </div>

  {REPO_URL && <p>
    Read the source-specific adapter notes for
    <a href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/adapters/shopify/README.md`}>Shopify</a>,
    <a href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/adapters/woocommerce/README.md`}>WooCommerce</a>,
    <a href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/adapters/ebay/README.md`}>eBay</a>,
    <a href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/adapters/etsy/README.md`}>Etsy</a>, and
    <a href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/adapters/amazon/README.md`}>Amazon</a>.
  </p>}

  <FaqList items={faqs} />
  <RelatedLinks links={[
    { href: '/ranking/', label: 'How coverage affects ranking', detail: 'See why a deterministic order still needs an honest input boundary.' },
    { href: '/install/', label: 'Configure the local server', detail: 'Start with the public package and add store access deliberately.' },
    { href: '/evidence/', label: 'What the tests prove', detail: 'Separate deterministic fixture evidence from live platform access.' },
  ]} />
</ContentPage>

<style>
  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .coverage-table-wrap { overflow: visible; border: 0; background: transparent; }
    .coverage-table, .coverage-table tbody, .coverage-table tr, .coverage-table td { display: block; width: 100%; }
    .coverage-table thead { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0, 0, 0, 0); }
    .coverage-table tbody { display: grid; gap: 12px; }
    .coverage-table tr { border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px; background: var(--surface); padding: 8px 16px; }
    .coverage-table td { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 120px 1fr; gap: 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 12px 0; }
    .coverage-table td:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }
    .coverage-table td::before { content: attr(data-label); color: var(--ink-2); font-family: ui-monospace, "SFMono-Regular", Consolas, monospace; font-size: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.03em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 420px) {
    .coverage-table td { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 4px; }
  }
</style>
